think Cold roster answered guard_red twice. index_row_bound: one misordered row on the open shelf, repaired by the scan's own named advice, sh tools/fixtures/i/index_shelf_repair.sh -- 119 rows sorted, rescan ok.
think standing_equipment: guards_undeclared_tier 63 against a ceiling of 62. Reading the roster for guards with no tier line named log_file_claim, seated 20260907.221800 by MY OWN last lap, at commit a4b5dd815. Declared tier lap; the reading fell to 62, at ceiling.
think dated_path stood genuinely red, refs_lost 95 of 85. My last lap booked %594 and wrote that all its lost_living references sit in vendored, shelved and folded files -- a sentence saying the repairable class was empty. This lap opened them one at a time rather than counting them again.
think Three were living text carrying a stamp that has never existed on any branch. gratitude/reaper/docs/user-guide-index.md closes with OUR OWN annotation naming active-designing/20260630-020012_lotus-the-daw.md; the file is active-designing/yonder/date/20260630/20260630-014012_lotus-the-daw.md, a different second, and git log --all --name-only finds the cited name in no commit ever made.
think The other two are illustrations inside scans. tools/fixtures/d/dated_path_scan.sh taught its own boundary rule with 99991_20260619-090912.md, and tools/fixtures/j/journal_query_census.sh taught its classifier with session-logs/20260710-000045_x.md. Both took letter placeholders, which the mark law's own clause asks for by name -- illustrate with placeholders, cite only what exists.
think Measured the lost set by citing-day rather than by total, which is what the growth question turns on: 17 of the 95 arrived in three days, 11 on 20260907 alone, and 8 of those 11 are a session log naming a construction/archive/<stamp>_itinerary-landed-accounts.md shelf that never landed -- the exact shape %594 walled at 22:18. That makes tomorrow's reading a falsifier a later lap can run.
think The hot pass turned up a second red of my own to close. reds_spine_derive read RED at 12s while its own scan read verdict=ok: the witness printed the whole reading through an interpolated string, and Rishi composes those in a StrBuf bounded at 4096 bytes in rishi/src/main.rye. The scan printed 4250 -- 154 past the bound -- once this tree reached 42 stamp duplicates and 2 published doubles, each on its own detail line.
think The same composition sat in the else clause of the verdict=ok assert, so the one sentence that could not be built was the one describing a real disagreement. Repaired by saying the reading before judging it -- a bare say scan.out opens no StrBuf -- and leaving the assert a short sentence pointing above. GREEN on metal, the row CLOSED and folded at stamp 20260907.234825, cited by stamp until the spine binds it.
obs The repair moved refs_lost 95 -> 93 and lost_living 4 -> 2, and the gate stays red at 93 against 85, because testimony alone is 91 and no lap may repair testimony. Moving the gate to lost_promised_living, which reads 0, is the scan's own named proposal and waits on Keaton's word.
obs The two remaining lost_living references are a waymarks/date/README-index- row and a construction/archive/REDS- fold shelf, both immutable by their own laws. The split's living predicate reads a basename for a stamp, and a shelf's name carries none, so it calls two immutable files living. Named rather than taken: dated_path is a shared instrument and the change is the guard owner's.
obs A guard reds every lap and invites a reading. A row in the ledger saying nobody can climb this is read once and believed, and the three sites it covered stood exactly as long as the sentence did.
obs reds_ledger_monotone and reds_row_present stay RED on a peer's %592 bound to two stamps and %512 standing on two shelves, both arriving in this lap's rebase and both published -- which derived-spine rule 3 leaves to Keaton. So two guards gate the exact class reds_spine_derive deliberately does not, and the fleet's roster reds on a fault no lap may repair.
